run 1, I'll rely on the aggregate statistics already in the JSON for runs 2-4 and use the paper's reported figures as reference for the first run. This gives me a complete picture for Table 2 without needing to reconstruct every individual data point.\n\nNow I'm ready to write the paper with a clear structure—starting with an abstract that includes appropriate hedging, organizing around the C3 findings as the main narrative, and including all the tables from both experiments.\n\nFor the tables, I'm planning: aggregate metrics across the 7B BCP runs, a failure mode replication summary, the 14B DPO+BCP results, a scale-dependent failure mode matrix as the centerpiece, and a bootstrap analysis comparing the two model sizes.\n\nI'm checking whether the project already has LaTeX style files available before deciding on the template format.\n\nNow I need to double-check my calculations for the multi-run statistics—verifying the mean retention rates and standard deviations for FM1 across the four runs to ensure accuracy in the tables. Now I'm checking the FM3 rebound values and confirming the mean and standard deviation calculations match up. Moving into the FM4 pooled bootstrap results, I'm seeing the gap differences for both AAVE and Indian groups are small and not quite reaching significance, with p-values around 0.08.\n\nFor the 14B model, the gap differences drop even further—0.018 for AAVE and 0.016 for Indian—suggesting the effect largely disappears at larger model sizes. Comparing across model sizes, the 7B single-run estimate shows roughly an 8.6x larger gap difference than the 14B results, though I need to be careful about whether I'm comparing the pooled bootstrap value or the original single-run baseline.\n\nNow I'm clarifying which evidence to prioritize for the paper. The original MVP run shows AAVE completion dropping to 0.100 versus a baseline of 0.368, with Indian at 0.150. The politeness findings show substantial drops in both question generation and resp